# ABSTRACT

Lithium-sulfur (Li-S) battery has attracted intensive attention in the realm of energy storage owing to its high theoretical capacity and energy density. However, the shuttle effect of soluble lithium polysulfides (LiPSs) between electrodes results in rapid capacity degradation. Herein, a strategy which combines the design of both chemical interaction and microstructure of interlayer was proposed to suppress the shuttle effect. The chemical interaction between different functionalized MOFs and LiPSs was systematically studied to find the best candidate. Furthermore, an interlayer with ordered structure was constructed via the layer-by-layer assembly of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) on graphene (URC66-NH\(_{2}\)@graphene) to create sinuous channels which can better impede the diffusion process of LiPSs by the strong adsorption of MOF toward LiPSs. Consequently, in comparison to the battery with a bare separator, the ordered interlayer increased the initial discharge capacity of battery by 28.98% at 1.0 C and lowered the capacity decay rate remarkably from 0.10% to 0.067% per cycle, indicating that the design of chemical interaction and microstructure paves the way for high-performance Li-S batteries.

# KEYWORDS

ordered structure, interlayer, metal-organic frameworks, lithium-sulfur battery

# 1 Introduction

Lithium-sulfur (Li-S) battery has become one of the most promising rechargeable batteries owing to its high theoretical capacity \((1,672\mathrm{\mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}})\), high energy density \((2,600\mathrm{\Wh{\cdot}kg^{-1}})\), low cost and environmental friendliness [1–3]. However, its practical application has been limited by self-discharging, low Coulombic efficiency, poor cycling stability and loss of active sulfur material, which are mainly caused by the shuttle effect of intermediate soluble LiPSs \((\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{x},\ 4\leq x\leq8)\). Specifically, during the charge/discharge process of Li-S battery, the soluble LiPSs may penetrate through the separator, diffuse to the Li metal anode, react with Li metal and eventually form insoluble \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) and \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}\) [4]. To address the shuttle effect, researchers have proposed solutions based on the design of sulfur host, interlayer, separator, binder and electrolyte [5–9].The design strategies of addressing shuttle effects can be mainly divided into three categories, i.e., physical barrier, chemical adsorption and catalytic conversion [10]. Physical barrier can block the LiPSs via small pore size or charge repulsion [11, 12]. Chemical adsorption can trap LiPSs via specific chemical interaction and thus suppress shuttle effects. For instance, porous carbon materials can serve as S host in cathode [13–15], and doped carbon materials can serve as interlayer to capture LiPSs [16]. Functionalized BN Nanosheets, polar materials such as \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) can interact with LiPSs and hinder their diffusion [17, 18]. Catalytic conversion mainly relies on different catalysts such as dithiothreitol, hemin molecules, iron phthalocyanine and octafluoronaphthalene, which can promote the conversion of LiPSs [19–21]. Our former researches mainly concentrate on suppressing shuttle effects via adsorption and catalytic conversion toward LiPSs, and it was found that adsorption is important for both of these two strategies [13–21]. However, traditional porous materials such as carbon materials and metal oxide, have weak interaction toward LiPSs, which cannot obstruct the diffusion of LiPSs efficiently and finally lead to decay of battery performance, especially for battery with high sulfur loads [22, 23]. The inefficient adsorption ability of these porous materials is restricted by the scarce adsorption sites. As a result, developing porous materials with high-density adsorption sites to trap LiPSs remains still a great challenge.Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), composed of organic linkers and metal ion (or metal clusters), have been widely used in various applications [24–29]. By virtue of the large pore volume which can store LiPSs and the devisable adsorption sites for LiPSs, MOFs have also been applied as interlayers for LiPSs. For example, ZIF-8, which have smaller aperture sizes than LiPSs were investigated as interlayer to restrain the shuttle effect with the size exclusion effect [30]. However, the MOF-based interlayers are plagued by defects caused by the heterogeneous structure, through which the LiPSs can diffuse

