# 2.6 Diffusion coefficient of lithium ion

The \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) diffusion coefficient \((D_{\mathrm{Li^{+}}})\) was measured by cyclic voltammetry (CV) with different scan rates of 0.2, 0.3, 0.4 and \(0.5\,\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) and calculated based on the Randles–Sevcik equation

\[
I_{\mathrm{p}}=2.69\times10^{5}\times n^{3/2}AD_{\mathrm{Li^{+}}}^{1/2}\nu^{1/2}C_{\mathrm{Li^{+}}}
\]

where \(I_{\mathrm{p}}\) indicates the peak current, \(n\) is the number of electrons in the reaction, \(A\) is the electrode area, \(\nu\) is the scanning rate, and \(C_{\mathrm{Li^{+}}}\) is the \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) concentration in the electrolyte.

# 2.7 Material characterization

The powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) were recorded by a Rigaku SmartLab X-ray diffractometer in a \(2\theta\) range of \(5^{\circ}\) to \(30^{\circ}\). The scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images were obtained with Hitachi S4800 electron microscope (Japan). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements were conducted with Thermo Escalab 250 system. TG analysis of the CNT/S composite was performed in a PerkinElmer (TA Instruments) up to \(600~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) with a heating rate of \(10\ {}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\cdot\mathrm{min}^{-1}\) in \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\). The adsorption capability of MOFs toward \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{6}\) was characterized with the ultraviolet–visible (UV–vis) spectrometer (UV-2600).

# 2.8 Fabrication of Li-S battery and the electrochemical measurements

CR2032 Li-S coin cells were assembled using CNT/S as the cathode, functionalized GF as the separator and lithium as the anode. \(1\,\mathrm{M}\) lithium bis(trifluoromethane sulfonimide) (LiTFSI) in DOL and DME mixture (1:1 by volume) with 1 wt \(\%\) LiNO3 was used as electrolyte. The ratio of electrolyte to S for S/CNT cathode was controlled to be \(20~\upmu\mathrm{L}\cdot\mathrm{mg}^{-1}\). The galvanostatic charge-discharge tests were carried out at a voltage range of \(1.5{-}2.8\,\mathrm{V}\) using a Neware battery testing system. The CV measurements and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) were conducted with an electrochemical workstation (Autolab 302N) and the CV measurements were carried out from 1.5 to \(2.8~\mathrm{V}\) at a scan rate of \(0.1\ \mathrm{mV}\cdot\mathrm{s}^{-1}\). All the electrochemical tests were performed at room temperature.

# 3 Results and discussion

In this work, the UiO series of MOFs were selected as MOF candidates because of their physicochemical stability and chemical tunability [31]. Specifically, a series of Zr-MOFs which have the same structure but different functional groups, i.e., –OH, \(\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) and –COOH, can be easily synthesized for investigating the influence of functional groups on the trap of LiPSs and performance of battery. The crystallinity of the obtained MOFs was firstly testified by the PXRD (Fig. S2 in the ESM) and the difference in ligand did not influence the crystal structure significantly. However, as shown in Fig. 2(a), MOFs with different functional groups demonstrate different adsorption capabilities toward LiPSs, and it seems that UiO\(66{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}\) performed the best as the \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{6}\) solution with UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) as adsorbent became almost clear after \(5~\mathrm{min}\). UV–vis absorbance spectroscopy in Fig. 2(b) further verifies this observation. The peaks at 260 and \(280\;\mathrm{nm}\) are assigned to the absorption of \(\mathrm{S}_{6}{}^{2-}\) in \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{6}\), and therefore, the least amount of \(\mathrm{S}_{6}{}^{2-}\) was left in the solution after the adsorption with UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), indicating that \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) has the highest adsorption capacity for LiPSs. It is also need to be noted that all the functionalized MOFs, i.e., UiO-66-2OH, UiO-66-COOH

