and \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) demonstrate higher adsorption efficiencies compared with UiO-66. Considering that the functionalized MOFs have smaller surface areas due to the presence of the functional groups in pores, the improved adsorption properties thus can be ascribed to the interaction between LiPSs and Lewis bases in MOFs, i.e., –OH, –COOH and \(\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) groups.

XPS was applied to investigate the interaction between LiPSs and MOFs taking \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) as an example. In Fig. 2(c), the peak centered at \(53.9~\mathrm{eV}\) in Li 1s spectrum of graphene/ \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{6}\) is attributed to the Li-S bond. Whereas after polysulfide adsorption, the collected spectrum of \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}\) shows an asymmetric Li 1s peak that can be deconvoluted into 2 peaks, as shown in Fig. 2(d). According to previous reports [35–37], the additional peak centered at \(56.2~\mathrm{eV}\) can be attributed to the \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) interacting with N. In the N 1s spectra of \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) (Fig. S3(a) in the ESM), the peak centered at \(399.4~\mathrm{eV}\) in N 1s spectrum is attributed to the \(\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) group [38]. Whereas after adsorption of polysulfide, the collected spectrum of \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}/\) \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{6}\) shows an asymmetric N 1s peak that can be deconvoluted into 2 peaks. According to previous reports [36], the additional peak centered at \(398.9~\mathrm{eV}\) can be attributed to the interaction between \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) and N. For \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{6}\), two sets of doublets located at \(163.3/164.4~\mathrm{eV}\) and \(161.3/162.8~\mathrm{eV}\) can be observed (Fig. S3(b) in the ESM), representing the atoms of bridging sulfur \((\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{B}}{}^{0})\) and terminal sulfur \((\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{T}}{}^{-1})\) [39]. After adsorption, no obvious shift can be observed for the S 2p doublets. After adsorption, blue shift can be observed for the S 2p doublets, implying the interaction between S and \(\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) groups. Therefore, the plentiful amino groups in \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) are expected to facilitate the adsorption of LiPSs via the strong polar interaction with \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\), explaining the highest adsorption.

As \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) demonstrates the best LiPSs adsorption capability, it was applied in the interlayer of Li-S battery. Graphene can be directly used as interlayer by virtue of its two-dimensional (2D) structure and physical barrier effect for LiPSs, and thus was investigated as a reference in this work. In order to investigate the influence of structure design of interlayers, \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}/\) graphene and \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) graphene were prepared. \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}/\) graphene was obtained through vacuum filtration of slurry containing dispersed MOF particles and graphene, a typical preparation route for MOF-based interlayer. Our innovation here is to construct ordered \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) graphene as interlayer in which
MOF particles were grown directly on graphene to obtain UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene and UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene was then assembled layer-by-layer on GF separator. The PXRD result in Fig. S4 in the ESM verifies the crystallinity of UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene, resembling that of UiO-66-NH\(_2\).

The enlarged cross-sectional views of both physically mixed UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene and ordered UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene are shown in Fig. S5 in the ESM, which show a thickness of 1.3 and \(1.5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\), respectively. It can be observed in Fig. 3(a) that UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene has a disordered structure as the UiO-66-NH\(_2\) nanoparticles agglomerated severely and a mass of gaps were present between the 2D graphene sheets, which is detrimental for the blocking of LiPSs. In contrast, the UiO-66-NH\(_2\) particles in ordered structure of UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene distributed uniformly on graphene sheets without agglomeration (Fig. 3(b)), and the cross-sectional view in Figs. 3(c) and 3(d) further indicates that a closely packed structure was realized with MOF particles residing between orderly arranged graphene sheets. As a result, LiPSs can only circuitously diffuse along the interlamellar space between the graphene sheets, which will greatly prolong the diffusion route of LiPSs. Moreover, according to our design scheme in Fig. 1, during the diffusion process, LiPSs are expected to be trapped by the highly adsorptive UiO-66-NH\(_2\).

