The rate performances of Li-S batteries fabricated with different separators were evaluated, which are shown in Fig. 5(a). The initial discharge capacity of \(1598\,\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) for UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) graphene interlayer was delivered at \(0.2\,\mathrm{C}\) ( \(1.0\,\mathrm{C}=\) \(1,675\,\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}})\) . With increasing the current density to \(0.5\,\mathrm{C},\) \(1.0\,\mathrm{C},2.0\,\mathrm{C}\) and \(3.0\,\mathrm{C},\) discharge capacities of 1,100, 900, 725, \(705\,\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) were obtained, respectively. In comparison, the battery with UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) /graphene as interlayer displayed discharge capacities 1,400, 900, 760, 700 and \(500\,\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) at \(0.2\,\mathrm{C},\,0.5\,\mathrm{C},\,1.0\,\mathrm{C},\,2.0\,\mathrm{C}\) and \(3.0\,\mathrm{C},\) respectively, lower than that of UiO- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) graphene. The batteries with graphene/GF and pristine GF separator displayed even lower discharge capacities. Compared with the interlayer with disorder structure, the interlayer with ordered structure can improve the discharge capacity by \(41\%\) at \(3\,\mathrm{C}.\) In addition, the batteries with ordered interlayers constructed with different MOFs were also investigated (Fig. S7 in the ESM). The performance of the battery with UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) is superior than the that of the batteries with other MOFs, which matches well with the best adsorption capacity of \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) . Therefore, the improved barrier effect of the interlayer restrained the shuttle process of LiPSs and leads to significant improvement of the rate performances.

The cyclic voltammetry curves of all cells shown in Fig. 5(b) exhibit two obvious reduction peaks at \(\sim\,2.3\) and \(\sim\,2.0\,\mathrm{~V},\) corresponding to the transformation from \(S_{8}\) to long-chain polysulfides and the further reduction of polysulfides to \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}.\) . It can be seen that UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) graphene had more positive potentials during the reduction process and more

negative potentials during the oxidation process compared with UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) /graphene and graphene interlayer, indicating the smallest polarization of the cell with UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) graphene. The results are compatible with the voltage hysteresis \((\Delta E)\) in the charge-discharge profiles (Fig. 5(c)). The \(\Delta E\) of the cell with UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) graphene is \(0.25\,\mathrm{eV},\) smaller than that of 0.33 and \(0.31\,\mathrm{eV}\) with UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) /grapheme and graphene interlayer, respectively. These results demonstrate better inhibitory effect of ordered UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) graphene interlayer for shuttle effect during the discharge of Li-S batteries.

The self-discharge test was conducted to further investigate how the composite separators influence the performance of Li-S batteries in Fig. S8 in the ESM. The self-discharge, which is resulted from the shuttle of soluble LiPSs, leads to irreversible decay of specific capacity and reduction of the open-circuit voltage (OCV). The Li-S batteries with graphene, UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}/\) graphene and UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) graphene as interlayer were tested by discharging to \(2.15\,\mathrm{~V~}\) and resting for \(10~\mathrm{{h}}\) in the \(2^{\mathrm{nd}}{-}4^{\mathrm{th}}\) cycles. After the \(4^{\mathrm{th}}\) charge, the batteries were rested for \(72~\mathrm{h}\) and the OCV reduction was studied. Compared with the other batteries, the discharge specific capacity of the battery with UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) graphene as interlayer displays the lowest decrease \((8.20\%)\) and displays a higher initial voltage \((2.33\mathrm{~V})\) after the \(4^{\mathrm{th}}\) cycles (Fig. S8(c) in the ESM). These results imply that the ordered structure of layered UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) graphene has excellent anti-self-discharge behavior by effectively suppressing the shuttle effect of LiPSs.

To further investigate the efficiency of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) diffusion in UiO66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) graphene and UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}/\) graphene. The Li-ion diffusion coefficient \((D_{\mathrm{Li^{+}}})\) was measured by CV at different scan rates of 0.2, 0.3, 0.4 and \(0.5\ \mathrm{mV{\cdot}s^{-1}}\) and calculated based on the Randles–Sevcik equation [41]. As shown in Fig. S9 in the ESM, the peak currents increase with the increasement of scan rate. All the peak currents of cathodic and anodic have a linear relation with the square root of scan rates \(\left(\upsilon^{1/2}\right)\) . The calculated diffusion coefficients are listed in Table S1 in the ESM, demonstrating that the battery with UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) graphene as interlayer shows higher \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) diffusion coefficients. These results show that the interlayer of UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) graphene has the best block effect without hampering the diffusion of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) .

